ries\\\", bracket format) could silently break the parser for that one path without a test catching it. Not yet confirmed to currently diverge -- would need a byte-level comparison across all 3 generators' output plus targeted round-trip tests to confirm live vs. latent.\\n\\n## Finding 2: Ordinal-path format has 1 independent regex validator, several independent string-formatting generators\\n\\nGenerator (produces ordinal path strings via plain f-string composition):\\n- backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py _entry_ordinal_for_sub_heading() -> f\\\"{parent}.{idx}\\\"\\n- backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py _entry_ordinal_for_code() -> f\\\"{parent}.code.{k}\\\"\\n\\nValidator (independently hand-written regex describing the same shape):\\n- backlog_core/disclosure_handler.py _ORDINAL_PATTERN = re.compile(r\\\"^\\\\d+(\\\\.\\\\d+)*(\\\\.code\\\\.\\\\d+)?$\\\")\\n\\nInspection did not find a live mismatch (the regex currently accepts every shape the generator functions can produce), but the regex is not derived from or tested against the generator -- a change to either side could silently desynchronize them, same risk class as #2956's fixed _normalize_section_key/parse_issue_body mismatch.\\n\\n**Research first**: for Finding 1, consider extracting one canonical render_section_index_line(idx, title, count, is_groomed) function all 3 generators call, and a matching parse_section_index_line() the parser uses -- mirroring the rendering.heading_to_unknown_key single-source-of-truth pattern #2956 established. For Finding 2, add a hypothesis property test asserting every ordinal string ordinal_mapper.py can generate matches _ORDINAL_PATTERN (cheaper than a full refactor, and would have caught #2956-shaped drift automatically). Confirm current behavior with fresh reads of all 3 generators' outputs before choosing an approach.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n#